= Cardiac output — Technical =

== Flow-rate definition ==
Cardiac output CO is a volume flow rate, most often left ventricular volume ejected per minute:

<pre>
CO = HR × SV
SV = EDV - ESV
</pre>

HR is heart rate, SV stroke volume, EDV end-diastolic volume and ESV end-systolic volume. If HR has units beats/minute and SV mL/beat, CO has units mL/minute. Values should be time-averaged with a specified observation interval.

== Hemodynamic models ==
A lumped steady-flow approximation of the systemic circulation can express:

<pre>
mean arterial - mean venous pressure ≈ CO × systemic vascular resistance
</pre>

This is not an equation for systolic or diastolic blood pressure. Pulsatility, vascular compliance, nonuniform flow and measurement conditions are not captured by the simplified expression.

== Measurement uncertainty ==
Invasive methods, ultrasound-based estimates and other approaches differ in how they infer volume flow. Measurement quality can depend on calibration, geometry, rhythm, operator technique and physiological assumptions. A pulse rate alone lacks the required stroke-volume information.

